1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The ancient Egyptians saw — a
star that woke the Nile.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Egypt gets almost no rain. Life depends
on the Nile.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Sirius, which the Egyptians called
Sopdet, sinks into the sunset glow each
spring.

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Around then the river began to rise. The
Egyptian year originally began that day.

5
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Pyramid age, Memphis: Sirius returned a
few days after the solstice, just in
time for the flood.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Today in Cairo, Sirius waits until
around 5 August.

7
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
In 1970 the Aswan High Dam was finished.
The yearly flood now stays in a
reservoir.

8
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Around 10 p.m., face south: Sirius
stands at its highest, with Orion to its
upper right.

9
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Ancient Arabia: a desert zoo in the sky.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

